.\" Manual page for swaptop. View with: man -l docs/swaptop.1
.TH SWAPTOP 1 "October 2026" "swaptop 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
swaptop \- htop\-style view of which processes are using swap, and how
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swaptop
[\fB\-d\fR \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fICOLUMN\fR] [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-z\fR]
.br
.B swaptop
\fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B swaptop
is a full\-screen terminal monitor, laid out like
.BR htop (1),
that breaks swap usage down by process and by kind. It answers three
questions: how much swap is in use, which processes own it, and how much of
that is really swapped out to the device rather than merely reserved.
.PP
It reads
.IR /proc/meminfo ,
.IR /proc/swaps
and, for every process,
.IR /proc/PID/status ,
.IR /proc/PID/smaps_rollup
and
.IR /proc/PID/smaps .
Nothing is written anywhere except the configuration file (see
.BR FILES ).
Actions that change memory placement are available from the function keys
and always ask for confirmation first.
.PP
Running as root (\fBsudo swaptop\fR) is recommended: without it the kernel
hides the detailed figures and memory of other users' processes (see
.BR PERMISSIONS ).
.SH OPTIONS
Every option overrides the value loaded from the configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-d " SECONDS"
Refresh interval. Fractions are allowed. Default 2.
.TP
.BI \-s " COLUMN"
Sort column, one of
.BR SWAP ,
.BR SWPPSS ,
.BR DELTA ,
.BR RSS ,
.BR SWAPPED ,
.BR PID ,
.BR USER ,
.BR COMMAND .
Default \fBSWAP\fR.
.TP
.B \-t
Start in tree view, processes arranged under their parents.
.TP
.B \-z
Show every process, not only those with memory in swap.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a short usage message and exit.
.SH DISPLAY
.SS Header
.TP
.B Swp
Swap in use out of the total, as a bar coloured by kind:
.RS
.TP
green
attributed to processes.
.TP
yellow
swap cache: pages that are in RAM \fIand\fR still hold a swap slot. They
have been read back in (or were written out ahead of time) and the slot is
kept so they need not be written again. This is "reserved" swap: it costs
nothing to drop and the kernel does so as soon as the device fills up.
.TP
magenta
owned by no process: swapped tmpfs and shared memory files, or slots whose
owner cannot be read without root.
.RE
.TP
.B Mem
RAM in use, buffers and page cache, as in
.BR htop .
.TP
.B zswap
Shown when zswap is active: how much of the used swap is actually held
compressed in RAM rather than on the device.
.TP
Devices
One line per entry in
.IR /proc/swaps
with size, usage and priority. zram devices also show the uncompressed and
compressed sizes.
.TP
Commit
Committed address space against the commit limit, then the number of
processes with memory in swap and the current sort order.
.SS Columns
.TP
.B PID
Process ID.
.TP
.B USER
Owner.
.TP
.B SWAP
Swapped memory this process maps: its own anonymous pages plus any shared
memory (shmem, tmpfs, SysV) it has mapped. Shared memory is counted in every
process that maps it.
.TP
.B SWPPSS
Proportional set size of the process's anonymous swap: pages shared with
other processes (after
.BR fork (2))
are split between them, so this column sums to the real total. Shared
memory files are not counted. Shown as \fB\-\fR when it cannot be read.
.TP
.B DELTA
Change in \fBSWAP\fR since the previous refresh. Red means the process is
being swapped out, green that it is coming back in.
.TP
.B RSS
Resident memory, in RAM.
.TP
.B SWAPPED
Share of the process's memory (\fBSWAP\fR plus \fBRSS\fR) that is in swap
right now, as a bar and a percentage.
.TP
.B COMMAND
Command name, or the full command line after pressing \fBc\fR. In tree
view it is indented under the parent process.
.SS Detail view
.B Enter
opens a breakdown of the selected process by memory mapping, sorted by
swap: heap, stacks, shared libraries, anonymous regions and files, each with
its swap, proportional swap and resident sizes. This shows \fIwhat\fR has
been swapped out, not just how much.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End
Move the selection.
.TP
.B Enter
Per\-mapping breakdown of the selected process. \fBEsc\fR or \fBq\fR returns.
.TP
.B F1, h, ?
Help.
.TP
.B F3, /
Incremental search over command line, name, user and PID. \fBEnter\fR keeps
the filter, \fBEsc\fR clears it.
.TP
.B F5, t
Toggle tree view. In tree view a process is shown when it or any of its
descendants matches, so parents stay for context; processes without swap are
dimmed.
.TP
.B F6, s
Cycle the sort column.
.TP
.B z
Toggle between processes with swap only (the default) and all processes.
.TP
.B c
Toggle between the command name and the full command line.
.TP
.B F10, q
Quit, saving the current settings.
.SH ACTIONS
Each action asks for confirmation before doing anything.
.TP
.B F7, i \(em swap in
Faults every swapped page of the selected process back into RAM by reading
it through
.IR /proc/PID/mem .
The pages to read are found with
.IR /proc/PID/pagemap
so nothing else is touched. Afterwards the process runs at full speed again.
Its swap slots usually remain allocated as swap cache (yellow in the bar)
until the kernel needs them or the device is flushed.
.TP
.B F8, o \(em swap out
Asks the kernel to reclaim the selected process's resident memory now, with
.BR process_madvise (2)
.BR MADV_PAGEOUT
(Linux 5.10 or later). Anonymous memory is written to swap; clean file\-backed
pages are simply dropped. The process keeps running and faults its memory
back as it touches it. Useful for parking an idle but large process.
.TP
.B F2, F \(em flush
Empties every swap device completely by calling
.BR swapoff (2)
and then
.BR swapon (2)
on each, in order. Everything in swap is pulled back into RAM and every slot,
including swap cache, is released.
.B swaptop
refuses if available RAM is smaller than the swap in use. Each device's
priority is kept; other
.BR swapon (8)
options such as discard are not reapplied. Needs root.
.TP
.B F9, k \(em signal
Opens a picker of common signals (TERM, KILL, INT, HUP, QUIT, USR1, USR2,
STOP, CONT) to send to the selected process.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Reading another user's
.IR smaps_rollup ,
.IR smaps ,
.IR mem
and
.I pagemap
requires ptrace access to that process, which on most systems
(\fIkernel.yama.ptrace_scope\fR = 1) means root or
.BR CAP_SYS_PTRACE .
Without it the header reports how many swapped processes could not be read,
their \fBSWPPSS\fR shows \fB\-\fR and their \fBSWAP\fR counts anonymous
memory only; swap in and the detail view fail with a hint to use
.BR sudo .
Swap out additionally needs
.BR CAP_SYS_NICE ,
and flush needs
.BR CAP_SYS_ADMIN .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from the file below at start\-up and written back when
.B swaptop
exits, so the sort order, tree view, zero\-swap filter, command\-line display
and refresh interval persist between runs. The file holds one
.I key = value
per line and may be edited by hand:
.PP
.nf
    sort = SWAP          # see \-s for the names
    hide_zero = true     # false shows every process
    full_cmd = false
    tree = false
    refresh = 2          # seconds
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/swaptop/config
Configuration, normally
.IR ~/.config/swaptop/config .
When run under
.BR sudo (8)
this is root's home unless \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is passed through.
.TP
.IR /proc/meminfo ", " /proc/swaps ", " /proc/PID/*
Data sources. See
.BR proc (5).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a normal quit, 1 if the terminal cannot be initialised, 2 on a bad
option.
.SH NOTES
Swap cache explains most apparent mismatches: a process can show 0 in
\fBSWAP\fR while the device's usage barely drops, because the slots are kept
until needed. Flushing (\fBF2\fR) is the only way to release them on demand.
.PP
The \fBSWAP\fR column of processes sharing memory (database workers, for
example) double counts that memory, by design, so that each process shows
what it would have to fault in. The header's "in processes" figure uses
\fBSWPPSS\fR and does not double count.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR htop (1),
.BR free (1),
.BR vmstat (8),
.BR swapon (8),
.BR proc (5),
.BR process_madvise (2)
